mpc2000xl: the record transfer's key-on, and a real input monitor

Bit 8 of the control register keys on m_cur_channel, and the firmware
sets it during a record transfer with no preceding voice select, so it
landed on whatever voice was selected last. When that picked 0x10 - the
right-channel record voice - the voice was left playing the wave RAM the
take was writing while still being a record destination.

Measured on :speaker after a take, per channel, against an idle baseline
of rms 78-89:

    LEFT   peak  744   rms  131.6   baseline, silent
    RIGHT  peak 8619   rms 1327.0   ~15x baseline, looping the take

A rate error or an xrun degrades both channels alike; only a voice left
running can be loud in one ear and silent in the other. The user heard it
as a ring buffer repeating, and only ever after recording.

Suppressing that key-on during a transfer makes takes record cleanly and
land under the pads. It also, on its own, killed the input monitor - the
accidental key-on was what had been making the input audible, which is
not something to rely on. So record_capture() now mixes the input into
the output deliberately, where it already reads it. Monitoring is a
property of the sampler being armed, not of a voice that happens to be
running.

The record voices are also keyed off when a take ends, so nothing is left
running across the boundary even if a key-on does slip through.

--- a/src/devices/sound/l7a1045_l6028_dsp_a.cpp
+++ b/src/devices/sound/l7a1045_l6028_dsp_a.cpp
@@ -691,7 +691,24 @@
 
 	m_control = data;
 
-	if (BIT(data, CONTROL_KEY_ON))
+	// BIT 8 IS NOT A KEY-ON WHILE A TAKE IS TRANSFERRING.
+	//
+	// The firmware sets it during a record transfer with NO preceding voice
+	// select (mov ah,1 / out dx,ax at 0x4965e), so it keyed on whatever voice
+	// was selected last - seen on ch 23, ch 12, ch 0 and ch 16 across runs.
+	// When the lottery picked 0x10, the RIGHT record voice was left playing
+	// the wave RAM the take was writing, while still being a record
+	// destination. Measured on :speaker after a take, against an idle
+	// baseline of rms 78-89: LEFT rms 131.6 (silent), RIGHT rms 1327.0,
+	// looping. Only a voice left running can be loud in one ear and silent in
+	// the other; a rate error or an xrun degrades both alike. With this
+	// suppressed, takes record cleanly and land under the pads correctly.
+	//
+	// It costs the input monitor, because that accidental key-on was also
+	// what made the input audible - so record_capture() now mixes the input
+	// into the output deliberately instead. Monitoring is a property of the
+	// sampler being armed, not of a voice that happens to be running.
+	if (BIT(data, CONTROL_KEY_ON) && !(m_rec_on && BIT(data, CONTROL_DMA_START)))
 	{
 		l7a1045_voice* const vptr = &m_voice[m_cur_channel];
 
@@ -922,6 +939,13 @@
 			if (q > 32767) q = 32767;
 			if (q < -32768) q = -32768;
 
+			// MONITOR: the input has to be audible while the sampler is
+			// armed, which is how a player sets the level at all. The real
+			// machine does this on its analog path; here it is a deliberate
+			// mix, added before the voice loop so a voice playing over the
+			// top still sums normally.
+			stream.add_int(ch, j, q, 32768);
+
 			m_rec_ring[m_rec_wr & (REC_RING - 1)] = int16_t(q);
 			m_rec_wr++;
 			if (!m_dump_prefix.empty() && (m_dump_in.size() < 40u * 44100u * 2u))
@@ -1062,6 +1086,26 @@
 	}
 	if (!on && m_rec_on)
 		dump_take();
+	// THE TAKE IS OVER: SILENCE THE VOICES IT KEYED ON.
+	//
+	// The firmware keys a voice on during a record transfer, and that
+	// key-on is what makes the input audible - suppressing it, which was
+	// the first attempt at this bug, killed monitoring outright. The fault
+	// is not the key-on but that nothing ever keys the voice off again: it
+	// is left running over the wave RAM the take just wrote, looping it.
+	//
+	// It is worse than a stuck note because the transfer programs voice 0
+	// and voice 0x10 as the two halves of a stereo take, so the voice left
+	// playing is a record destination as well - a Pi trace caught posR
+	// advancing at twice pos0 for exactly that reason. Measured on the
+	// desktop after a take, :speaker per channel against an idle baseline
+	// of rms 78-89: LEFT rms 131.6 (baseline, silent) and RIGHT rms 1327.0,
+	// about fifteen times baseline, looping. Only a voice left running can
+	// be loud in one ear and silent in the other; a rate error or an xrun
+	// degrades both alike.
+	if (!on && m_rec_on)
+		m_key &= ~((1U << 0) | (1U << REC_RIGHT_VOICE));
+
 	m_rec_on = on;
 	m_rec_left = left;
 	m_rec_right = right;
